    "q-342281": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342281",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y=2{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi }(t)=u(t-\\pi )$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=1:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-1\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\pi $ and $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi ).$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-\\pi )\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )\\} & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L\\{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡((t+\\pi )-\\pi )\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L\\{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot \\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ (sY(s)-1)+Y(s) & =\\frac{2s{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s)-1 & =\\frac{2s{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =1+\\frac{2s{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{2s{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
