    "q-342762": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342762",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y={u}_{1}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>2$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+3y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{1}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-2}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{1}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-2}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=1$ and $f(t)={e}^{2t-2}.$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed exponential forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-1)\\cdot {e}^{2t-2}\\} & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{2(t+1)-2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{1}(t)\\cdot {e}^{2t-2}\\} \\\\ sY(s)+3Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s-2} \\\\ (s+3)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s-2} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{(s-2)(s+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
