    "q-342772": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342772",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\,,\\,y(0)=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{3}{s+2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+2y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-3:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-3)=sY(s)+3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $f(t)=5cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{5u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /4)s}\\cdot L\\{5cos\\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot L\\{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+3)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)+3 & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =-3+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{3}{s+2}+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
