    "q-342782": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342782",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y={u}_{4}(t)\\cdot (t-4)\\,,\\,y(0)=-1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{4}(t)=u(t-4)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{{e}^{-4s}}{{s}^{2}(s-2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-{e}^{2t}+u(t-4)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-4s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s-2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s-2)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s-2)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s-2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s-2)+B(s-2)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-2)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(4)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{4s}-\\frac{1}{2{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4(s-2)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{4s}-\\frac{1}{2{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4(s-2)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{t}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-4s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-4)f(t-4) & =u(t-4)(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{t-4}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2(t-4)}) \\\\  & =u(t-4)(\\frac{7-2t}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2(t-4)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-{e}^{2t}+u(t-4)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2(t-4)}+\\frac{7-2t}{4}.\n$$",
